posted_comments:
Thanks for the clear report — I reproduced this against the 1.4.2 checkout.

`minLength` is compared with JavaScript’s `String.prototype.length`, which counts UTF-16 code units, not user-perceived characters (grapheme clusters) or Unicode code points.

```js
export function validate(value, rules) {
  if (rules.minLength && value.length < rules.minLength) return {valid:false};
  return {valid:true};
}
```

So your call is working as the code is written today:

```js
validate('😀', { type: 'string', minLength: 2 });
// '😀'.length === 2 (one code point, two UTF-16 code units) → { valid: true }

validate('😀', { type: 'string', minLength: 1 });
// also { valid: true }

validate('ñ', { type: 'string', minLength: 2 });
// 'ñ'.length === 1 → { valid: false }
```

A few related cases, so the unit is obvious:

| value | `.length` (code units) | code points | graphemes you see |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `a` | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| `😀` | 2 | 1 | 1 |
| `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦` | 11 | 7 | 1 |

`type: 'string'` is currently ignored; only `minLength` is enforced, and only when it is truthy (so `minLength: 0` does not reject anything).

This is not a logic bug relative to the implementation, but it is a real footgun for display-name limits, and neither the README nor `docs/configuration.md` says which unit `minLength` uses. A short docs note is warranted — something like: “`minLength` / string length uses JavaScript UTF-16 code units (`String#length`), not grapheme clusters. Emoji and some other characters count as more than 1.”

I would not change the comparison itself in a patch release: callers may already depend on `.length`, and switching to code points or graphemes would be a breaking change. If we want a grapheme-aware limit later, that should be a new option (for example `minGraphemes`) rather than a silent change to `minLength`.

I also checked for a related #95 before writing this; that issue is not in the tracker, so there is nothing else to fold in. Happy to take a docs PR that adds the note above.

