Thanks for the clear repro — I confirmed this against the 1.4.2 checkout.

`minLength` uses JavaScript’s `String.prototype.length`, which counts UTF-16 code units, not user-perceived characters (grapheme clusters) or Unicode code points. The emoji `😀` is one code point (U+1F600) encoded as a surrogate pair, so `'😀'.length === 2`. That is why:

```js
validate('😀', { type: 'string', minLength: 2 });
// { valid: true }
```

A single BMP character such as `'a'` has length 1 and would fail the same rule. This matches ordinary JS string length; it is not a separate Lineguard counting mode.

A short docs note would help (the current README/config docs don’t mention length units), so I’ll treat a documentation clarification as warranted. Changing the count to code points or graphemes would be a breaking behaviour change and isn’t required to make the current rule correct — if you need a grapheme limit for display names, count with `Intl.Segmenter` (or `[...str].length` for code points) before calling `validate`, or set the limit in UTF-16 units to match what the library checks today.

