posted_comments:
Thanks for the clear report. I reproduced this on the 1.4.2 checkout:

```js
validate('😀', { type: 'string', minLength: 2 }); // { valid: true }
validate('😀', { type: 'string', minLength: 3 }); // { valid: false }
validate('a',  { type: 'string', minLength: 2 }); // { valid: false }
validate('ab', { type: 'string', minLength: 2 }); // { valid: true }
```

This is current, intended behavior, not a regression. `minLength` is implemented as a comparison against JavaScript's `String.prototype.length`:

```js
if (rules.minLength && value.length < rules.minLength) return { valid: false };
```

`length` counts UTF-16 code units, not user-perceived characters (grapheme clusters) and not Unicode code points. U+1F600 GRINNING FACE is one code point, but it is encoded as a surrogate pair, so `'😀'.length === 2`. That is why `minLength: 2` accepts it. The same rule applies to other non-BMP characters (many emoji, some CJK extension characters): one visible glyph can contribute 2 to `length`. Combining sequences can be even longer — for example a family ZWJ emoji is one grapheme but many code units.

ASCII and most BMP characters are one code unit each, which is why `'a'` fails `minLength: 2` and `'ab'` passes. The existing tests cover those ASCII boundaries only; they do not document the UTF-16 behavior.

A code change is not warranted for 1.4.x. Switching to code points (`[...value].length`) or grapheme clusters would change accepted inputs for anyone already relying on JS string length, and grapheme segmentation is not something this dependency-free library does today. If a display-name limit should mean "one emoji counts as one", that has to be applied by the caller (for example `Intl.Segmenter` with `granularity: 'grapheme'`) before or instead of `minLength`.

A documentation note is warranted. README does not currently say what `minLength` counts, which is exactly the gap in this report. I would add a short public-API note, something like: "`minLength` uses JavaScript string length (UTF-16 code units). Supplementary-plane characters such as emoji count as 2. It does not count Unicode code points or grapheme clusters." Happy to take a docs PR for that; behavior and tests can stay as they are unless we intentionally version a different counting mode later.

I also checked the open issue list. #94 is the only open report, so there is no duplicate to merge this with.

